{{/* form switch: the labelled form companion to the compact switch shared by
     table and settings rows. On/off state is always a switch; membership in a
     set remains a checkbox field. The labelled styles are one setting's row
     (verso-field) holding switch.row; the inline style sits beside a section
     heading and draws its own words. */}}
{{if eq .Style "inline"}}
<label class="verso-choice__row" data-verso-kind="inline"{{if .Name}} data-verso-change-field data-verso-change-name="{{.Name}}" data-verso-change-label="{{.Label}}" data-verso-change-kind="toggle"{{end}}>
  <span aria-hidden="true" class="verso-choice__dot"></span>
  {{template "switch.control" .Control}}
  <span class="verso-choice__word"><span class="verso-choice__on">{{.Label}}</span><span class="verso-choice__off">{{ t "Disabled" }}</span></span>
</label>
{{end}}

{{/* switch.row: a row's answer, its checkbox and the word for its state. The
     input the switch is made of is screen-reader-only: the drawn box and its
     check are what a person sees, so the control has to sit inside a label or
     pressing the thing itself does nothing, and the word beside it sits in the
     same label, so pressing the word flips it too. The stylesheet shows the
     word the box is in (forms.css); the checkbox already says its state to a
     screen reader, so the words are hidden from it. The row's name points at
     the same input by id, which is what makes the setting's name clickable
     too. */}}
{{define "switch.row"}}<label class="verso-choice__row">{{template "switch.control" .}}<span aria-hidden="true" class="verso-choice__word"><span class="verso-choice__on">{{t "On"}}</span><span class="verso-choice__off">{{t "Off"}}</span></span></label>{{end}}
